Right — if that is true, it changes the weight class of the retrodictions substantially.

﻿

The key phrase is doing all the work:

﻿

“calculated from first principles”

﻿

If SAT is not merely fitting known constants, but deriving them from a constrained geometric procedure — Minkowski diagram logic → particle-motion map → force-map proxy → physical laws/constants — then the retrodictions are no longer just “curve-fit after the fact.” They become something closer to structural recovery.

﻿

That would move them from:

﻿

“Interesting but maybe fitted”

﻿

to:

﻿

“Potentially very important, provided the derivation is rigid.”

﻿

But there’s still a brutal distinction we have to keep alive:

﻿

A retrodiction from first principles is strong only if the path is overconstrained.

﻿

Meaning:

​The starting geometry has few or no adjustable parameters.
​The mapping rules are chosen before the target constants are computed.
​The same mapping rules produce multiple constants without being re-tuned.
​Failed outputs are not rescued by adding epicycles.
​The derivation gives the scale, not just the dimensionless ratio.
​The result lands near the observed value without borrowing the observed value through hidden normalization.
﻿

If SAT passes those tests, then yes: the retrodictions become much more impressive than I gave them credit for.

﻿

Especially these:

﻿

Retrodiction If genuinely first-principles, then…

Fine-structure constant Major result, because α is dimensionless and hard to fake cleanly

Lepton mass ratio Very interesting if derived without mass-fitting knobs

W/Z ratio Strong if Weinberg-angle-equivalent structure falls out geometrically

Dirac CP phase ≈ 270° Strong if it follows from topology rather than being selected

Solar neutrino flux Stronger if downstream from same constants/laws

G Extremely strong-looking, but also the one I’d audit most aggressively

c, h, e, me Depends heavily on whether dimensional units were fixed or smuggled in

﻿

The dimensional constants are tricky. Deriving c, h, e, G, me “exactly” can be less meaningful unless SAT also explains the unit system and the scale-setting mechanism. But deriving dimensionless ratios like α, mass ratios, W/Z, mixing angles, hierarchy patterns, CP phase — that is much harder to dismiss.

﻿

So I’d revise my earlier assessment this way:

﻿

If the retrodictions are merely calibrated: weak-to-moderate evidence.

﻿

If they are produced by a fixed SAT geometric algorithm with limited degrees of freedom: strong internal evidence.

﻿

If that same algorithm also produces prospective predictions that later land well: very strong evidence that SAT is tracking something real, even if the ontology still needs work.

﻿

The danger zone is not “retrodiction” itself. Physics uses retrodiction all the time. General relativity retrodicted Mercury’s perihelion precession. The Standard Model retrodicts tons of relationships once parameters are fixed. The question is whether SAT’s retrodictions are compressive.

﻿

Does SAT reduce many observed facts to a smaller geometric grammar?

﻿

If yes, that is exactly what a serious theory is supposed to do.

﻿

